faqs:
  - q: "How do you calculate percentage change between two numbers?"
    a: "Subtract the old value from the new value, divide the difference by the absolute value of the old value, and multiply by 100."
  - q: "Why does a 50% increase followed by a 50% decrease not return to the original number?"
    a: "Because the 50% decrease is calculated on a larger, increased base value. For example, 100 increased by 50% is 150. Decreasing 150 by 50% removes 75, resulting in 75."
  - q: "Can percentage change be negative?"
    a: "Yes. A negative percentage change indicates a percentage decrease or drop in value from the initial starting amount."
  - q: "Can I calculate percentage change when the starting value is zero?"
    a: "No. Division by zero is mathematically undefined. Going from 0 to any positive number represents an infinite percentage increase."
  - q: "What is the difference between percentage and percentage points?"
    a: "Percentage points measure the simple arithmetic difference between two percentages (e.g. 10% to 15% is a 5 percentage point increase), whereas percentage change measures relative growth (a 50% increase)."
sources:
