      <h2 id="article-heading">How Do You Calculate Percentage Change Between Two Numbers?</h2>
      <p>To calculate percentage change between two numbers, subtract the old value from the new value, divide the difference by the absolute value of the old value, and multiply by 100. A positive answer indicates a percentage increase, while a negative answer indicates a percentage decrease.</p>

      <p>If you are measuring time intervals between project milestones or financial quarter start dates, try our <a href="/tools/date-difference-calculator/">Date Difference Calculator</a>.</p>

      <h3>How Do You Increase or Decrease a Number by a Percentage?</h3>
      <p>To increase or decrease a number by a percentage, calculate the percentage portion (multiply base number by percent rate divided by 100) and then add (for increases) or subtract (for decreases) that portion from the starting number.</p>

      <p>When preparing reports or financial summaries, you can quickly analyze word count and reading time using our <a href="/tools/word-counter/">Word &amp; Character Counter</a>, or clean up text formatting with our <a href="/tools/text-case-converter/">Text Case Converter</a>.</p>

      <div class="info-callout">
        <h3>Understanding the Reversibility Trap</h3>
        <p>A +50% increase followed by a -50% decrease does not return you to your original starting number. For example, $100 + 50% = $150. Decreasing $150 by 50% removes $75, resulting in $75.</p>
      </div>
